export const FIGURE_ITEMS_U4 = [
  {"id":"U4-D16","topic":"u4-nutrients","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"4.1/LO5","fig":"G-U4-nutcycle","q":"Label the stages of this marine nutrient cycle.","extra":["energy flowing back to the producers","nutrients used up and destroyed"],"why":"Producers take up dissolved nutrients in the sunlit surface water, and the nutrients pass along food chains. Dead organisms and faeces sink as marine snow, bacteria decompose them near the seabed and release the nutrients into the deep water, and upwelling carries that nutrient-rich water back to the surface. Energy is not recycled, and the nutrients are never destroyed: they go round and round."},
  {"id":"U4-D17","topic":"u4-nutrients","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.1/LO5","fig":"G-U4-nutcycle","target":"upwelling","q":"Tap the arrow that returns nutrient-rich water to the sunlit surface.","why":"Upwelling carries cold, nutrient-rich deep water up to the surface, where producers can use the nutrients again, so the cycle is complete. The arrow down from the dead material is marine snow sinking, and the arrow from the bacteria shows nutrients being released into the deep water, not carried up."},
  {"id":"U4-D18","topic":"u4-nutrients","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.1/LO5","fig":"G-U4-nutcycle-snow","q":"In this diagram, material from the box marked ? sinks through the water column as marine snow. What is marine snow?","options":["dead organisms, faeces and other organic waste sinking slowly through the water","ice crystals that form in the cold surface water near the poles and then sink to the seabed","salt crystals left behind when sea water evaporates at the surface, sinking to the seabed","fragments of the calcium carbonate skeletons of dead corals, broken up by waves and sinking"],"a":0,"why":"Marine snow is dead organisms, faeces and other organic waste drifting slowly down, so the box marked ? is the dead material from the producers and consumers above it. It carries nutrients to the deep, where bacteria release them. It is not ice, which floats rather than sinks; salt stays dissolved in sea water; and broken coral skeleton is sediment from reefs, not material that sinks through the open water column."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-01","topic":"u4-nutrients","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.1/LO2","fig":"G-U4-foodtest","target":"tubeD","q":"Two food samples, R and S, were tested with the reagents shown. Tap the tube that shows protein is present.","why":"Biuret solution is the test for protein: it starts blue and turns lilac (purple) when protein is present. Tube D is lilac, so sample R contains protein. Tube F also contains biuret solution but has stayed blue, a negative result for sample S. The brick-red tube is a positive Benedict's test, which shows reducing sugar, not protein."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-02","topic":"u4-nutrients","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"4.1/LO2","fig":"G-U4-foodtest","q":"Iodine solution was added to sample R in tube B. What does the result show?","options":["Sample R contains no starch.","Sample R contains starch.","The test failed, as the colour did not change.","Sample R contains no reducing sugar."],"a":0,"why":"Iodine solution is orange-brown, and turns blue-black only if starch is present. Tube B has stayed orange-brown, so sample R has no starch. No change is a valid negative result, not a failed test. The iodine test cannot show anything about reducing sugar; that needs Benedict's solution."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-03","topic":"u4-nutrients","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.1/LO4","fig":"G-U4-coral","target":"skeleton","q":"A coral polyp takes in calcium from sea water and uses it to build one part of its body. Tap that part.","why":"Calcium is used to build the hard calcium carbonate skeleton, the white cup the polyp sits in. Skeletons laid down by many polyps build up the reef. The tentacles, mouth and stomach are soft tissue, and the magnified stinging cells are part of a tentacle."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-04","topic":"u4-respiration","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.2/LO2 · 4.2/LO4","fig":"G-U4-resp-gasex","q":"Which picture shows the process that happens in every living cell?","options":["the left picture, showing respiration","the right picture, showing respiration","the right picture, showing gas exchange","the left picture, showing gas exchange"],"a":0,"why":"The left picture shows glucose and oxygen going in and carbon dioxide and water coming out: respiration, the reactions that release energy, which happen in every living cell. The right picture shows a fish gill, where oxygen passes from the water into the blood and carbon dioxide passes out: gas exchange. Gas exchange happens only at an exchange surface such as the gills. It supplies the oxygen for respiration, but the gills do not respire for the whole fish."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-05","topic":"u4-respiration","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"4.2/LO1","fig":"G-U4-pneumatophore","q":"These mangrove roots grow up out of the mud and take in oxygen from the air. Which process in the root cells uses this oxygen, and what does it release?","options":["respiration, which releases energy","photosynthesis, which releases glucose","gas exchange, which releases energy","respiration, which releases oxygen"],"a":0,"why":"The mud is waterlogged and short of oxygen, so these roots take oxygen in from the air. The root cells use it in respiration, which breaks down glucose and releases energy. Taking the oxygen in is gas exchange, but gas exchange only moves gases; it releases no energy. Respiration uses oxygen rather than releasing it, and photosynthesis uses light, not oxygen."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-06","topic":"u4-photosynthesis","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.3/LO2","fig":"G-U4-kelp","target":"blade","q":"Tap the part of this kelp that absorbs most of the light it uses for photosynthesis.","why":"The blades are large, thin and flat, so they have a big surface area to absorb light, and their cells contain chlorophyll in chloroplasts. The round gas bladders, the stipe and the holdfast do other jobs and absorb little of the light."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-07","topic":"u4-photosynthesis","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.3/LO2 · 3.5/LO2","fig":"G-U4-kelp","q":"The round gas bladders on this kelp help it to photosynthesise faster. How?","options":["They float the blades up near the surface, where the light is brighter.","They store carbon dioxide taken from the air, ready for the blades to use.","They hold most of the kelp's chlorophyll, so they absorb most of the light.","They store the oxygen the kelp needs to keep photosynthesising at night."],"a":0,"why":"The bladders are filled with gas, so they float the blades up towards the surface, where more light reaches them and photosynthesis is faster. Kelp takes in dissolved carbon dioxide over its blades, not from air stored in bladders. Most chlorophyll is in the blades. Photosynthesis needs light, so it stops at night, and it releases oxygen rather than using it."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-08","topic":"u4-photosynthesis","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.3/LO4","fig":"G-U4-producers","target":"diatom","q":"Tap the organism that photosynthesises.","why":"The diatom (bottom left) is a single-celled protoctist with a silica skeleton and golden-brown chloroplasts, so it makes its own food by photosynthesis. The copepod and the jellyfish are zooplankton, and the isopod is a seabed animal; all three are consumers that eat other organisms. Being plankton does not make an organism a producer."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-09","topic":"u4-photosynthesis","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.3/LO1","fig":"G-U4-resp-photo","q":"One of these pictures shows the part of a cell where oxygen is released as a by-product. Which picture, and which process releases the oxygen?","options":["the left picture: photosynthesis","the right picture: respiration","the left picture: respiration","the right picture: photosynthesis"],"a":0,"why":"The left picture is a chloroplast: it is green because it contains chlorophyll, which absorbs light for photosynthesis. Photosynthesis makes glucose from carbon dioxide and water and releases oxygen as a by-product. The right picture is not green and has no chlorophyll, so photosynthesis cannot happen there. Respiration uses oxygen; it does not release it."},
  {"id":"U4-D01","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"label","tier":1,"ref":"4.4/LO3","fig":"G-U4-foodweb-blank","q":"In this kelp-forest food web, sea urchins eat kelp and sea otters eat sea urchins. Zooplankton eat phytoplankton, herring eat zooplankton and seals eat herring. Orcas eat both sea otters and seals. Label every organism.","extra":["the Sun","dissolved nutrients"],"why":"Each arrow points from a food to the organism that eats it, so the two producers are the bottom boxes: kelp on the left (eaten by sea urchins, which are eaten by sea otters) and phytoplankton on the right (eaten by zooplankton, then herring, then seals). The orca is the top box, the only one with two arrows in, from the sea otter and the seal. The Sun and dissolved nutrients are not organisms, so neither has a box."},
  {"id":"U4-D02","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.4/LO7","fig":"G-U4-foodweb","target":"orca","q":"Tap the organism that feeds at more than one trophic level.","why":"The orca eats the sea otter, which is at trophic level 3 (kelp, sea urchin, sea otter), so there it feeds at level 4. It also eats the seal, which is at level 4 (phytoplankton, zooplankton, herring, seal), so there it feeds at level 5. Every other consumer has only one arrow pointing into it, so it feeds at one trophic level."},
  {"id":"U4-D03","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.4/LO4","fig":"G-U4-foodweb","q":"Disease removes all of the sea urchins from this web. Which organism is most at risk of losing its only source of food?","options":["the sea otter, because the sea urchins are its only food","the orca, because it eats the sea otters that feed on the urchins","the kelp, because it depends on the sea urchins","the herring, because it feeds on the sea urchins"],"a":0,"why":"The only arrow into the sea otter comes from the sea urchin, so it loses its only food. The orca will have fewer sea otters to eat, but it can still eat seals. The kelp is the sea urchins' food, not their predator, so it is likely to increase. No arrow joins the herring to the sea urchins."},
  {"id":"U4-D05","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.4/LO13","fig":"G-U4-pyr-numbers","target":"bar-l3","q":"Tap the bar showing the trophic level of the secondary consumer.","why":"The producer, phytoplankton, is always the bottom bar (trophic level 1). Zooplankton eat it, so they are the primary consumer (level 2). Sardines eat the zooplankton, so the sardine is the secondary consumer, at level 3: the third bar up. The top bar is the tuna, the tertiary consumer."},
  {"id":"U4-D06","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.4/LO12","fig":"G-U4-pyr-numbers","q":"In this pyramid each bar is narrower than the one below it. What best explains this?","options":["energy is lost at each transfer, so each level can support fewer organisms than the one below","the organisms at the higher levels live further from the sunlight, so fewer of them can survive","the organisms at higher levels reproduce more slowly, so fewer young are born at each level","predators kill more prey than they need to eat, so numbers fall towards the top"],"a":0,"why":"At each transfer, energy is lost in respiration, movement and excretion, and in parts that are not eaten or digested. Less energy reaches each level, so it can support fewer organisms. The consumers get their energy from food, not directly from sunlight; slow breeding is not the cause; and predators do not kill more than they need."},
  {"id":"U4-D07","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.4/LO11","fig":"G-U4-pyr-biomass","target":"step-producer","q":"Tap the trophic level with the greatest biomass.","why":"The bar widths are drawn to scale. The seaweed bar is 40 small squares wide, so its biomass is 40 × 20 = 800 g per m², against 280 g per m² for the limpets and 80 g per m² for the crabs. The producer has the greatest biomass, because only part of the material at each level becomes body mass at the next."},
  {"id":"U4-D09","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.4/LO12","fig":"G-U4-pyr-biomass","q":"Why is the biomass of the crabs so much smaller than the biomass of the seaweed?","options":["much of the food at each level is used in respiration and movement, or lost in excretion","each crab is a much smaller organism than each seaweed, so the crabs must have less biomass in total","biomass is destroyed when one organism eats another, so a fixed amount disappears at each step","the crabs return most of their biomass to the seaweed through the nutrient cycle, so the seaweed grows"],"a":0,"why":"At each level much of what is eaten is used in respiration and movement or lost in excretion, and some is never eaten or digested, so only a small part becomes biomass at the next level. Bar width shows the total biomass at a level, not the size of one organism. Material is not destroyed, and recycled nutrients do not explain the difference."},
  {"id":"U4-D11","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.4/LO13","fig":"G-U4-pyr-energy","q":"A pyramid of energy is always widest at the bottom and never inverted. Why?","options":["energy is lost at every transfer, so each level always contains less energy than the one below","there are always more producers than consumers in an ecosystem, so the base must be the widest bar","the producers are always the largest organisms in a food chain, so their bar is always the widest","respiration adds energy at each level, so energy builds up towards the bottom of the pyramid"],"a":0,"why":"Energy is lost at every transfer (in respiration, movement and excretion, and in parts not eaten or digested), so each level holds less energy than the one below and the pyramid can never be inverted. Producers are not always more numerous or larger: a pyramid of numbers can have a narrow base. Respiration releases energy from the organisms; it does not add any."},
  {"id":"U4-D14","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"tap","tier":1,"ref":"4.4/LO12","fig":"G-U4-energyflow","target":"arrow-on","q":"Tap the arrow that shows energy passed on to the next trophic level.","why":"Energy passed on goes from level A to level B: the thin arrow between the boxes. It is thin because only a small part of the energy taken in is passed on. The thick arrow from below is the energy taken in at level A, and the three arrows out of the side are energy lost."},
  {"id":"U4-D15","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.4/LO12","fig":"G-U4-energyflow","q":"The three arrows leaving the side of level A represent energy lost from this trophic level. Which processes do they represent?","options":["respiration, movement and excretion","photosynthesis, respiration and feeding","growth, reproduction and feeding","digestion, absorption and transfer to the next level"],"a":0,"why":"Energy is lost from a trophic level through respiration, movement and excretion. Photosynthesis is how energy enters a food chain, not how it is lost. Energy used for growth stays in the body and can be passed on when the organism is eaten, and feeding is how energy is taken in. The arrow to level B is the energy that is transferred."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-10","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":1,"ref":"4.4/LO8 · 4.4/LO9","fig":"G-U4-isopod","q":"This organism lives on the seabed and feeds on the remains of dead organisms and waste. Use the picture to decide which term describes it.","options":["a detritivore","a decomposer","a herbivore","a top predator"],"a":0,"why":"The picture shows an animal, an isopod, with jointed legs, antennae and eyes; an animal that eats dead or waste organic material is a detritivore. A decomposer, such as a bacterium, breaks dead material down without eating it. A herbivore eats living producers, and a predator catches and kills live prey."},
  {"id":"PQ-U4-11","topic":"u4-feeding","type":"choice","tier":2,"ref":"4.4/LO9","fig":"G-U4-detr-decomp","q":"Both pictures show dead material on the seabed being broken down. Which shows decomposers at work?","options":["the right picture only","the left picture only","both pictures","neither picture"],"a":0,"why":"In the magnified circle on the right, bacteria are growing over the dead kelp. They are decomposers: they break the dead material down without eating it and release its nutrients back into the water. The sea cucumber on the left is an animal that swallows dead material, so it is a detritivore, not a decomposer, which rules out both pictures. Dead kelp does not break itself down; it rots because decomposers feed on it."}
];
